EXCEL CTRL SHORTCUTS  (Windows)
Fundamentals: Most shortcuts work on a selected cell or range.
Ctrl = control key. Combine with letters, numbers, or Shift.

FILE / WORKBOOK
Ctrl + N          New workbook
Ctrl + O          Open workbook
Ctrl + S          Save
Ctrl + P          Print
Ctrl + W          Close workbook
Ctrl + F4         Close workbook (alternate)

EDIT / CLIPBOARD
Ctrl + C          Copy
Ctrl + X          Cut
Ctrl + V          Paste
Ctrl + Z          Undo
Ctrl + Y          Redo / Repeat
Ctrl + F          Find
Ctrl + H          Find & Replace
Ctrl + G          Go To
Ctrl + K          Insert hyperlink

FORMATTING
Ctrl + B          Bold
Ctrl + I          Italic
Ctrl + U          Underline
Ctrl + 5          Strikethrough
Ctrl + 1          Format Cells dialog
Ctrl + 2          Bold (same as Ctrl+B)
Ctrl + 3          Italic (same as Ctrl+I)
Ctrl + 4          Underline (same as Ctrl+U)

NUMBER FORMATS
Ctrl + Shift + $  Currency
Ctrl + Shift + %  Percentage
Ctrl + Shift + ^  Scientific
Ctrl + Shift + #  Date
Ctrl + Shift + @  Time
Ctrl + Shift + !  Number (comma + 2 decimals)
Ctrl + Shift + ~  General

DATA / FILL
Ctrl + D          Fill Down
Ctrl + R          Fill Right
Ctrl + E          Flash Fill
Ctrl + T          Create Table
Ctrl + L          Create Table (alternate)
Ctrl + Q          Quick Analysis
Ctrl + ;          Insert current date
Ctrl + Shift + :  Insert current time
Ctrl + '          Copy formula from cell above
Ctrl + Enter      Fill selection with same value

NAVIGATION / SELECTION
Ctrl + A          Select All / current region
Ctrl + Home       Go to A1
Ctrl + End        Go to last used cell
Ctrl + Arrow      Jump to edge of data
Ctrl + Space      Select entire column
Shift + Space     Select entire row
Ctrl + Page Down  Next worksheet
Ctrl + Page Up    Previous worksheet

HIDE / STRUCTURE
Ctrl + 9          Hide selected rows
Ctrl + 0          Hide selected columns
Ctrl + Shift + 9  Unhide rows
Ctrl + Shift + 0  Unhide columns
Ctrl + -          Delete cells / rows / columns
Ctrl + Shift + +  Insert cells / rows / columns

Notes
- Line break inside a cell is Alt + Enter (not Ctrl + J)
- New worksheet is Shift + F11 (not Ctrl + M)
- Works in most modern Excel versions on Windows
